% ============================================================================
% muscle-types — the three types of muscle tissue and the ONE distinctive
% characteristic the source assigns each one (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A rung a MEDICINE agent reasons UP from: below the four basic tissue types
% (see tissue-types.adj) the MUSCLE tissue splits into exactly THREE kinds —
% skeletal, smooth, and cardiac — and the source separates them by a single
% telling trait. In paraphrase (not the page's words): skeletal muscle is under
% voluntary control; smooth muscle is involuntary; cardiac muscle is the one
% with intercalated disks. Before you
% can reason about a muscle biopsy, a heart, or a gut wall you must first know
% WHICH of the three muscle-tissue types you are looking at. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? muscle_trait(cardiac, $Trait)      % intercalated_disks
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `muscle_trait(muscle, trait)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the trait
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word
% that is not one of these three muscle-tissue types (it never invents a trait).
%
% Each of the three muscle-tissue types and the ONE distinctive characteristic
% the source states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     muscle type   distinctive characteristic   a beginner's cue
%     -----------   --------------------------   ------------------------------
%     skeletal      voluntary                    the muscle you move on purpose
%     smooth        involuntary                  gut & vessel walls, not willed
%     cardiac       intercalated_disks           disks the heart sentence names
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a trait and recall its muscle type:
%
%     ? muscle_trait($Muscle, involuntary)   % smooth
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three muscle-tissue types — `bone`, `tendon`,
% `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a muscle recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a trait. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the muscle→trait pairs
% it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `trait` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% distinctive characteristic the source EXPLICITLY states for that muscle type,
% and the three values are kept DISTINCT. The page calls smooth muscle
% involuntary ("They are called involuntary muscles.") and says of cardiac
% muscle "Its contraction is not under voluntary control." So `involuntary`
% could not tell those two apart; cardiac's row therefore uses
% `intercalated_disks`, which the cardiac sentence names. The original header
% added that the source names
% intercalated disks as UNIQUE to cardiac muscle, quoting "The presence of
% intercalated disks distinguishes cardiac muscle as unique among the three
% types" as the source's own summary. UNVERIFIED: that quote does not occur on
% the cited page (measured 2026-09-15). Every row value stays literally
% checkable against its own quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every muscle→trait pair
% below was read out of a fetched NCI SEER Training Modules page, and any pair
% whose trait could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair,
% with the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states
% it:
%
%   skeletal → voluntary
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, multinucleated, striated, and
%      under voluntary control."
%
%   smooth → involuntary
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, have a single, centrally located
%      nucleus, and lack striations. They are called involuntary muscles."
%
%   cardiac → intercalated_disks
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Cardiac muscle has branching fibers, one nucleus per cell, striations,
%      and intercalated disks."
%
% (The original header said the three-type framing is stated on the same SEER
% page, quoting "There are three types of muscle tissue: Skeletal muscle ...
% Smooth muscle ... Cardiac muscle ...". UNVERIFIED: "There are three types of
% muscle tissue" does not occur on the cited page (measured 2026-09-15).)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). This paragraph
% used to say an ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope; per-row blocks
% exist, and the envelope was the skeletal sentence, so it was the primary
% source of all three answers. Each row quote above names its muscle type and
% its trait, so every row now takes its own as a `source`. The smooth row's is
% the two-sentence span, because "involuntary" is only in the second sentence
% and its "They" needs the first. The envelope is now the page's framing
% sentence, which names no muscle type and no trait:
%
%     "Muscle tissue is composed of cells that have the special ability to
%      shorten or contract in order to produce movement of the body parts."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the three row spans, the envelope, and the cardiac
% sentence quoted above ("Its contraction is not under voluntary
% control.") each occur exactly once, each inside a `<p>`. Every row's page
% is the envelope's, so no
% row restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4). All sources
% are the NCI SEER Training Modules, a primary U.S. government (National
% Cancer Institute) teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table muscle_trait {
    columns muscle, trait

    row (skeletal, voluntary) {
        source "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, multinucleated, striated, and under voluntary control."
    }
    row (smooth, involuntary) {
        source "Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, have a single, centrally located nucleus, and lack striations. They are called involuntary muscles."
    }
    row (cardiac, intercalated_disks) {
        source "Cardiac muscle has branching fibers, one nucleus per cell, striations, and intercalated disks."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE SKELETAL ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all three answers. It is now a framing sentence that names no muscle type
    % and no trait. Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "Muscle tissue is composed of cells that have the special ability to shorten or contract in order to produce movement of the body parts."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
